Of course I forgot to write up the armour weights when I checked my
copy of Ffoulkes's "Armourer and his craft" (a bit dated book but I
believe that he didn't misquote the weights of the armor suits) so now
I have to resort to my imperfect memory.

Ffoulkes gives weight of several harnesses that were made in the early
16th century. A "bullet-proof" armour weighted somewhere around 70
pounds where "bullet-proof" means just that: before the cuirass was
delivered a pistol was discharged at it from short distance to prove
that it didn't penetrate. (Usually only the breastplate was proven,
and the backplate as well as arm and leg guards were not).

By the 16th century armour was already significantly heavier than it
was in the Middle Ages. Most links that I could quickly google gives a
plate armour weight at 20-25 kg. Moreover, well-made plate armour is
actually *less* cumbersome to wear than mail because its weight
is better distributed than a hauberk's. 

I'm not aware of any medieval source that would attribute a lack of
manouverability for armour. Sure, there were situations like at
Agincourt where knights had severe troubles in getting back up after
they fell from their horses but that was more because of the soft
ploughed ground and cramped conditions. 

However, Ffoulkes quotes a few 16th century sources that speak about
the organisation of tournaments mentioning that it was commonplace to
build scaffolds to make mounting horses easier for jousters. But
again, this was the really heavy reneissance tournament armor, not the
lighter medieval combat plate, and the sources don't claim that it was
"impossible" to climb directly to saddle, only that it was more
convenient to use scaffolds.

