Well, one of my long-term projects has been to make a mail hauberk. I
made the mistake of using a little too large link size so I have to
find some way to strenghten the shoulders before I can really finish
and use it (thus far I have learned that: 1) soldering the links
doesn't work, 2) gluing the links doesn't work, and 3) I don't seem to
be able to visit the only store in Helsinki that sells key rings with
wholesale prices when it is open).

That particular hauberk is large enough to fit a haqueton inside and
it extends to my knees as and the sleeves are up to elbows. It weights
slightly less than 8 kg and has 10,500 links. I'd guess that a
well-made medieval hauberk of the same size would weight less than
that and would have about 16,000 links. (The difference in weight
coming from the fact that I used 2,000 of, now what are they called in
English, the round bits that you put between the base of a screw and
the hole, that weight much more than ordinary wire links.)

And of course, a medieval armoursmith would make the mail out of
riveted links, not butted as I have. The main difference in this case
is durability: butted links are easy to break while riveted links last
practically forever (or at least until the mail rusts away). 

So, coming back on topic I could say that I've managed to create an
orcish chain mail: heavy and of poor quality. (Though, if somebody
teleported me into a medieval battle field, I would much rather wear
it and the haqueton than be there without any protection at all). 


